  "visualDNA": {
    "Overview": "In his 1938 book Pillar to Post: The Pocket Lamp of Architecture, Lancaster commented that although Tudor buildings were on the whole cramped and ill-lit, they were regarded by many in the early 20th century as picturesque: \"so deep and so widespread was the … devotion to the olde-worlde that an enormous number of such houses were erected, at considerable expense\"."
